.\" Man page for sshcheck
.\" Contact: rom@romab.com
.TH SSHCHECK 1 "February 2026" "sshcheck 2.0.0" "Security Audit Tools"
.SH NAME
sshcheck \- SSH Security Audit Client for testing login capabilities
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sshcheck
[\fB\-t\fR \fIHOST\fR | \fB\-T\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-u\fR \fIUSER\fR | \fB\-U\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-p\fR \fIPASS\fR | \fB\-P\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-try\-empty\fR]
[\fB\-\-user\-as\-pass\fR]
[\fB\-\-port\fR \fIPORT\fR | \fB\-\-port-file\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-c\fR \fICMD\fR]
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sshcheck
is a security audit tool designed for testing SSH login capabilities across multiple hosts.
It attempts to authenticate to target SSH servers using provided credentials and captures
initial server output including welcome messages, banners, and shell prompts.
.PP
The tool provides host key fingerprinting, SSH algorithm enumeration with weak algorithm
detection, OS/version fingerprinting from SSH banners, automatic severity scoring of
findings, post-authentication command execution, and multiple output formats including
styled HTML reports.
.PP
This tool is intended for authorized penetration testing, security assessments, and
network audits. Always ensure you have proper authorization before scanning any systems.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Configuration"
.TP
.BR \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Load configuration from YAML or JSON file. All scan parameters can be specified
in the config file. CLI arguments override config file values.
.SS "Target Specification"
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-target " " \fIHOST\fR
Target host(s) to scan. Can be specified multiple times. Accepts:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Single IP address (e.g., 192.168.1.1)
.IP \(bu 2
CIDR range (e.g., 192.168.1.0/24)
.IP \(bu 2
IP range (e.g., 192.168.1.1-254)
.IP \(bu 2
Hostname (e.g., server.example.com)
.RE
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-target-file " " \fIFILE\fR
File containing target hosts, one per line. Supports the same formats as \fB-t\fR.
Lines starting with # are treated as comments.
.SS "Username Specification"
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " " \fIUSER\fR
Username(s) to try. Can be specified multiple times for multiple usernames.
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-user-file " " \fIFILE\fR
File containing usernames, one per line. Lines starting with # are treated as comments.
.SS "Password Specification"
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-password " " \fIPASS\fR
Password(s) to try. Can be specified multiple times for multiple passwords.
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-password-file " " \fIFILE\fR
File containing passwords, one per line. Lines starting with # are treated as comments.
.TP
.BR \-\-try\-empty
Also try an empty/null password for each username. Useful for detecting accounts
that allow passwordless login.
.TP
.BR \-\-user\-as\-pass
Also try the username as the password for each user. Tests for the common
misconfiguration where the password equals the username.
.SS "Port Specification"
.TP
.BR \-\-port " " \fIPORT\fR
SSH port(s) to try. Default is 22. Can be specified multiple times.
.TP
.BR \-\-port-file " " \fIFILE\fR
File containing ports, one per line. Lines starting with # are treated as comments.
.SS "Output Options"
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Save results to the specified file. The output format is determined by the \fB-f\fR option.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " \fIFORMAT\fR
Output format for the results file. Valid options:
.RS
.IP \fBtext\fR
Human-readable text format (default)
.IP \fBjson\fR
JSON format with full scan details, host key info, algorithms, and severity
.IP \fBcsv\fR
Comma-separated values for spreadsheet import
.IP \fBxml\fR
Structured XML format for tool integration
.IP \fBhtml\fR
Styled HTML report with summary cards, severity badges, and color coding
.RE
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Enable verbose output with detailed error messages and debug information.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress progress output, only show final summary.
.SS "Scan Control"
.TP
.BR \-\-stop\-on\-success
Stop testing a host:port combination after the first successful login is found.
Saves time on large scans where only one valid credential per host is needed.
.TP
.BR \-\-max\-attempts\-per\-user " " \fINUM\fR
Maximum number of failed password attempts per username per host before skipping
further attempts for that user. Protects against account lockout on systems with
lockout policies. Set to 0 for unlimited (default).
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-command " " \fICMD\fR
Execute the specified command on each successful login and capture the output.
The command is run via exec_command. When this option is used, the initial shell
output capture is skipped in favor of command output.
.TP
.BR \-\-checkpoint " " \fIFILE\fR
Save scan progress to a JSON checkpoint file. Progress is saved periodically
during scanning and when the scan is interrupted with Ctrl+C.
.TP
.BR \-\-resume " " \fIFILE\fR
Resume a previously interrupted scan from a checkpoint file. Already completed
attempts are skipped and previous results are restored.
.SS "Performance Options"
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-threads " " \fINUM\fR
Number of concurrent threads. Default is 1 (sequential scanning).
Higher values increase scan speed but may trigger rate limiting.
Recommended range: 1-50.
.TP
.BR \-\-timeout " " \fISECONDS\fR
Connection timeout in seconds. Default is 10 seconds.
Increase for slow networks or decrease for faster scanning.
.SS "Other Options"
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help message and exit.
.SH SECURITY FEATURES
.SS "Host Key Fingerprinting"
For each successful connection, sshcheck collects the server's host key type
(e.g., ssh-ed25519, ssh-rsa), SHA-256 fingerprint, and key size in bits.
Weak host keys such as DSA and short RSA keys are flagged in the severity assessment.
.SS "Algorithm Enumeration"
The tool reports available key exchange, cipher, MAC, and host key algorithms.
Weak algorithms are identified, including: RC4/arcfour ciphers, CBC mode ciphers,
MD5 and SHA-1 based MACs, DSA host keys, and weak Diffie-Hellman groups.
.SS "OS/Version Fingerprinting"
SSH banners are parsed to identify the operating system (Ubuntu, Debian, FreeBSD,
CentOS, Windows, etc.), OS family, and SSH software version. Known vulnerable
OpenSSH versions are flagged with appropriate severity levels.
.SS "Severity Scoring"
Each finding is automatically assessed for severity:
.TP
\fBCRITICAL\fR \- Root/admin login with default, empty, or username-as-password credentials.
.TP
\fBHIGH\fR \- Successful login for non-privileged users.
.TP
\fBMEDIUM\fR \- Weak host keys (DSA, short RSA), vulnerable SSH versions.
.TP
\fBLOW\fR \- Weak algorithms supported (RC4, 3DES, MD5-based MACs).
.TP
\fBINFO\fR \- No significant security findings.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
Configuration files support all scan parameters. CLI arguments override config values.
.PP
JSON example:
.PP
.nf
{
  "targets": ["192.168.1.0/24"],
  "users": ["root", "admin"],
  "passwords": ["password", "changeme"],
  "threads": 10,
  "try_empty": true,
  "user_as_pass": true,
  "stop_on_success": true,
  "max_attempts_per_user": 3,
  "command": "id; uname -a",
  "format": "html",
  "output": "report.html"
}
.fi
.PP
YAML configuration files require the \fBpyyaml\fR package.
.SH INPUT FILE FORMATS
All input files (targets, usernames, passwords, ports) support:
.IP \(bu 2
One entry per line
.IP \(bu 2
Lines starting with # are treated as comments
.IP \(bu 2
Empty lines are ignored
.IP \(bu 2
Leading and trailing whitespace is stripped
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one successful login was found.
.TP
.B 1
No successful logins found or error occurred.
.TP
.B 130
Scan was interrupted by user (Ctrl+C). Checkpoint is saved if configured.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Scan single host with single credentials:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 -u admin -p password123
.TP
Test empty passwords and username-as-password:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 --try-empty --user-as-pass -u root -u admin
.TP
Execute command on success with HTML report:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 -u root -p pass -c "id; uname -a" -f html -o report.html
.TP
Scan subnet with lockout protection:
.B sshcheck -t 10.0.0.0/24 -U users.txt -P passwords.txt -n 10 --max-attempts-per-user 3
.TP
Scan with checkpoint and resume:
.B sshcheck -T targets.txt -U users.txt -P passwords.txt --checkpoint scan.json
.br
.B sshcheck -T targets.txt -U users.txt -P passwords.txt --resume scan.json
.TP
Use configuration file:
.B sshcheck --config scan_profile.yaml
.SH ERROR MESSAGES
.TP
.B "Authentication failed"
The username/password combination was rejected by the server.
.TP
.B "Connection timed out"
The host did not respond within the timeout period.
.TP
.B "Connection refused"
SSH service may not be running on the specified port.
.TP
.B "No route to host"
Network path to the target is unavailable.
.TP
.B "Network is unreachable"
Cannot reach the target network.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I examples/targets.txt
Example target hosts file
.TP
.I examples/users.txt
Example usernames file
.TP
.I examples/passwords.txt
Example passwords file
.TP
.I examples/scan_profile.json
Example JSON configuration file
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.PP
This tool is designed for authorized security testing only.
Unauthorized use against systems you do not own or have explicit
permission to test may violate computer crime laws.
.PP
Passwords provided on the command line may be visible in process
listings. Use password files (\fB-P\fR) for sensitive credentials.
.PP
Checkpoint files contain scan results including passwords. Store
them securely and delete after use.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.IP \(bu 2
Python 3.6 or later
.IP \(bu 2
paramiko >= 2.7.0 (pip install paramiko)
.IP \(bu 2
pyyaml >= 5.0.0 (optional, for YAML config files)
